Posted by `coordinator-claude-c4b0c164-20261023` at Samuel's direction (2026-10-25).

## Goal
Review Lance Fortnow's expository rewrites of the OpenAI math papers ([fortnow/AI-Papers](https://github.com/fortnow/AI-Papers)) and his catalogue of the TCS results in the openai/math release ([OpenAI Math: TCS Selection](https://lance.fortnow.com/openai-math-tcs.htm); 40 TCS families and 73 papers as of 2026-10-15). Then extract into complexitylib the results that fit this library's scope: the headline theorems, and also the minor lemmas and techniques that are useful in their own right.

The rewrites so far cover the Unique Games Theorem, L = RL = BPL, and perfect completeness for 2-to-1 games. Other catalogued TCS families include:
- hardness of coloring 3-colorable graphs;
- a superquadratic separation of sensitivity and block sensitivity;
- depth-3 circuit lower bounds and average sensitivity of PTFs;
- permanent and iterated-matrix-multiplication lower bounds and a matrix multiplication exponent bound;
- Weisfeiler–Leman refinement complexity;
- one-tape time–space simulation;
- two-way automata state costs;
- uniform sparsest cut and bin packing hardness.

## Caution
These manuscripts come from an unreleased model, and the catalogue says they are "at different stages of verification". Some have Lean formalizations in the openai/math repository and some do not.
- **Proofs:** complexitylib proves every statement against its own definitions. A source formalization is a reference, not evidence. Unrefereed results are labelled as such.
- **Statements:** use exact statements from the cited manuscript, with the version or date pinned.
- **Code reuse:** openai/math is licensed under Apache 2.0 (checked at commit `1581d4cb`, 2026-10-12), as is complexitylib, so adaptation with attribution is permitted. The preferred practice is still to read it for the mathematics and write proofs against complexitylib's own definitions and conventions, for style and upstreamability (see #474). Any code that is adapted keeps Apache 2.0 attribution. The rewrites in fortnow/AI-Papers state no license and are read only.

## Milestones
- **M0, triage** (a comment here). A table of every TCS family with:
  - scope fit, from core to out of scope;
  - prerequisites already in the library, and links to existing tracks (for example #459/#474 for L = RL = BPL, #886 for the games results, #891 for algebraic lower bounds);
  - estimated size;
  - verification status (formalized at the source or not);
  - reusable minor parts.

  Samuel sets the priorities from it.
- **M1 onward:** one sub-issue per selected result, each with its own short design before any Lean.

Docstrings and citations for the classes area (#610 item 11). Only `/-- -/` and `/-! -/` text changes; no statement, proof, name, attribute or import does.

**Docstrings.** Adds docstrings to 60 public theorems that their module docs list as main results but that had none. They cover:
- communication protocols, affine dispersers, the Boolean hierarchy, EXP, graph non-isomorphism, the IP = PSPACE words and oracle TQBF;
- PH, promise classes, RP, generalized geography, TFNP, FPT/XP, TQBF and 3SAT;
- Kolmogorov complexity, language slices, and the Tree Evaluation and USTCON readers.

**Citations.** Cites sources in the docstrings of 47 complexity-class definitions, 24 decision-problem definitions and 13 NP-completeness headlines, using only existing `references.bib` keys:
- textbooks: Arora–Barak, Sipser, Papadimitriou and Garey–Johnson;
- Karp for Karp's problems;
- the original papers for the classes. Examples are Gill for PP/BPP/ZPP, Stockmeyer and Wrathall for PH, Fenner–Fortnow–Kurtz for SPP/WPP/LWPP/C=P, Blass–Gurevich for US/USAT, Megiddo–Papadimitriou for TFNP and Reingold for USTCON.

Section and theorem numbers appear only where the module already cites them or the chapter is certain. Works newly cited by a docstring are added to its module's References list.

**References sections.** Adds a `## References` section to 10 modules that had none:
- the Tree Evaluation reader (Cook–Mertz, Goldreich's Algorithm 1) and the Tree Evaluation encodings;
- Cook–Levin correctness;
- the short descriptions behind the hierarchy theorems;
- the words of the IP = PSPACE verifier;
- query-complexity restrictions.

**Two corrections.**
- `Knapsack.lean` credited the weight-value knapsack problem to Karp. Karp's KNAPSACK is the subset-sum form, so the problem is now credited to Garey–Johnson.
- `SubsetSum.lean` cited Sipser Section 7.4 for SUBSET-SUM, which Sipser introduces in Section 7.3.

**Left out.**
- *Files under other PRs:* the deprecation sweep #631, #632 and #612.
- *No confident source:* `E`, `NE`, complement classes such as `coUP`, the oracle variants of the counting classes, `UNSAT`, and `ExactThreeSAT_npComplete`. The last is the relaxed variant with repeated literals, which no source states.

**Overlap.** 22 of the files are also in my #634, #635, #646 or #620, but those PRs touch only declaration lines or other doc lines. Whichever lands second rebases.

**Validation:**
- `scripts/check.sh --quick --base origin/main` at 69b90760: every affected module and its clients build, and `mk_all --check` passes;
- a targeted build of the two modules changed in fb0603a2;
- `lake exe lint-style`;
- `python3 -B scripts/check_hygiene.py --check-simp`.

**Abstract impact.** None; documentation only.

